          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "PERIMETER AND AREA"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.327,
            0.327,
            0.351
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6.1 Perimeter"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.364,
            0.833,
            0.409
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Do you remember what the perimeter of a closed plane figure is? Let us refresh our understanding!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.411,
            0.835,
            0.525
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The perimeter of any closed plane figure is the distance covered along its boundary when you go around it once. For a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "polygon",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", i.e., a closed plane figure made up of line segments, the perimeter is simply the sum of the lengths of its all sides, i.e., the total distance along its outer boundary."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.53,
            0.833,
            0.553
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The perimeter of a polygon = the sum of the lengths of its all sides."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.559,
            0.833,
            0.605
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us revise the formulas for the perimeter of rectangles, squares, and triangles."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.627,
            0.416,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Perimeter of a rectangle"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.651,
            0.833,
            0.696
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Consider a rectangle ABCD whose length and breadth are 12 cm and 8 cm, respectively. What is its perimeter?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.697,
            0.785,
            0.744
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Perimeter of the rectangle = Sum of the lengths of its four sides = AB + BC + CD + DA"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "index": 11,
